00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
robert cantor was addicted to opioids  for three years  it nearly killed him all my  relationships collapsed  my finances collapsed my health  everything on wednesday  faced with mounting lawsuits johnson and  johnson  the largest maker of prescription  opioids plus three other of the largest  distributors  agreed to a proposed 26 billion dollar  settlement  announced by a coalition of state  attorneys general  today's action sends a message to drug  distributors and pharmaceutical  companies  that we simply will not accept this type  of behavior  opioids are a class of drugs naturally  found in opium  poppy plants that are then used in  illegal street drugs like heroin  but also in some prescription medicines  like highly addictive painkillers  such as oxycontin and vicodin and  fentanyl  a synthetic opioid as much as 100 times  more potent  than morphine america is in the middle  of an opioid epidemic  a half million people died from opioid  drug  overdoses in the last 20 years  93 000 last year alone  and states say it's the pharmaceutical  companies  that produce the opioids that are mostly  to blame  not only for the loss of life but also  for the huge  financial strain that it puts on local  governments  and their health systems in a statement  the company said it had deep sympathy  for everyone affected by the opioid  endemic  but added the settlement is not an  admission of  any liability or wrongdoing and the  company would continue to defend  against any litigation that the final  agreement  does not resolve i don't think that  26 billion is enough just to end the  challenges the united states has with  addiction and overdose but it could save  a lot of lives  as for cantor he feels the settlement  now is  better than going through more lengthy  litigation  it's a victory because we have to  balance the amount that these  distributors and johnson johnson are  willing to pay versus the amount of time  that's going to be lost so we can get  these dollars for treatment  it might not be the end of the  settlements prosecutors say they're  investigating other companies  with involvement in opioids and are  leaving the door open  to filing more lawsuits gabriel zondo  al jazeera new york
 

